From: SP11 Audio Reverse Engineering <sp11-audio@local>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 02:15:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 0072] ASoC: lpass-wsa-macro: materialize SP11 Windows TOP_CFG1

Native Windows writes WSA macro TOP_CFG1=0x03 after each enabled VI pair.
Linux carried 0x03 as a regmap default, but the SP11 cache policy can leave the
physical register unwritten.

The difference is observable before SP_VI: Golden v32 produces first-valid TAP2
in I,V,I,V physical-lane order, while native Windows produces V,I,V,I.
Materializing TOP_CFG1=0x03 on Denali changes the Linux producer itself to
Windows V,I,V,I without changing q6apm/SP_VI channel mapping.

A downstream SP_VI channel reorder was explicitly rejected. Once TOP_CFG1 was
also materialized it became a double correction and reproduced right-amplifier
err0=0x20 fault/static failures at 40/50%. Keep the correction at the WSA
producer boundary where Windows performs it.

Signed-off-by: SP11 Audio Reverse Engineering <sp11-audio@local>
---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-wsa-macro.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-wsa-macro.c
@@ -1562,6 +1562,10 @@
 		snd_soc_component_update_bits(component, tx_reg1,
 					      CDC_WSA_TX_SPKR_PROT_RESET_MASK,
 					      CDC_WSA_TX_SPKR_PROT_NO_RESET);
+
+		/* Windows qcaucd materializes TOP_CFG1 after each enabled VI pair. */
+		if (of_machine_is_compatible("microsoft,denali"))
+			snd_soc_component_write(component, CDC_WSA_TOP_TOP_CFG1, 0x03);
 	} else {
 		snd_soc_component_update_bits(component, tx_reg0,
 					      CDC_WSA_TX_SPKR_PROT_RESET_MASK,
